 "stimulus_html": "<p>“It would be an unspeakable tragedy if these countries, which have struggled so long against overwhelming odds, should lose that victory for which they sacrificed so much. Collapse of free institutions and loss of independence would be disastrous not only for them but for the world. Discouragement and possibly failure would quickly be the lot of neighboring peoples striving to maintain their freedom and independence.</p>\n<p>Should we fail to aid Greece and Turkey in this fateful hour, the effect will be far reaching to the West as well as to the East.</p>\n<p>We must take immediate and resolute action. I therefore ask the Congress to provide authority for assistance to Greece and Turkey in the amount of $400,000,000 for the period ending June 30, 1948.”</p>\n<p align=\"right\">President Harry S. Truman, <i>The Truman Doctrine Speech</i>, March 12, 1947</p>\n<p><i>Refer to the excerpt provided.</i></p>\n",
 "questions": [
  {
   "id": 616294,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "President Truman’s speech was most likely intended to increase the public’s awareness of",
   "answers": [
    {
     "id": 1173143,
     "content_html": "rising tensions over oil reserves in the Middle East"
    },
    {
     "id": 1173144,
     "content_html": "the Cold War and the struggle against Communism in Europe"
    },
    {
     "id": 1173142,
     "content_html": "the United States’ need for access to the Black Sea"
    },
    {
     "id": 1173141,
     "content_html": "the need to rebuild Europe after World War II"
    }
   ],
   "hints": [],
   "formats": [
    "multiple-choice"
   ],
   "combo_choices": []
  },
  {
   "id": 616295,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "The immediate outcome of the event described in the excerpt was that",
   "answers": [
    {
     "id": 1173147,
     "content_html": "the United States unilaterally rebuilt Europe"
    },
    {
     "id": 1173146,
     "content_html": "worldwide freedom of the seas was guaranteed for all nations"
    },
    {
     "id": 1173148,
     "content_html": "the United States’ foreign policy of containment was successfully implemented"
    },
    {
     "id": 1173145,
     "content_html": "Europe was not as vital to U.S. interests as initially believed"
    }
   ],
   "hints": [],
   "formats": [
    "multiple-choice"
   ],
   "combo_choices": []
  },
  {
   "id": 616296,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "Based on the ideas in the excerpt, which of the following observations of U.S. foreign policy in the post–World War II years is true?",
   "answers": [
    {
     "id": 1173152,
     "content_html": "The United States was making a major shift in foreign policy from its stance after World War I."
    },
    {
     "id": 1173151,
     "content_html": "More people opposed the idea of U.S. involvement in world affairs."
    },
    {
     "id": 1173150,
     "content_html": "A majority believed that U.S. foreign policy was being dictated by the United Nations."
    },
    {
     "id": 1173149,
     "content_html": "The United States needed to reassert the “Good Neighbor Policy,” but with a focus on Europe."
    }
   ],
